ltp: don't discard segments of a block that has no block file yet

acceptRedContent() treats blockFileRef == 0 as "the block file was destroyed",
but blockFileRef is also 0 before the file is first created: the receiver keeps
the first maxAcqInHeap bytes (heapmax, default 560) of each block in the heap
and only creates the block file when data goes past them. So when the first
segment of a block fits in the heap, every later segment is discarded as
"late", no report is sent, and the sender cancels the session.

Seen with a max segment size under about 575 bytes at the default heapmax, and
with any segment size once heapmax is raised (heapmax 65536, segments of 1,400:
nothing delivered). A destroyed file ref still leaves fileBufferPath set, so
checking it keeps the late-segment protection added in 3180084.

Against nasa-jpl/ION-DTN integration (c5a4d87 tested; the line is unchanged on
integration as of 2026-10-22). Reproduction: dtn-custody-demo, voyager/ION-RESULTS.md.

--- a/ltp/library/libltpP.c
+++ b/ltp/library/libltpP.c
@@ -6813,7 +6813,12 @@
 		 *	destroyed (set to 0 by clearImportSession), the
 		 *	file is gone and no further writes are possible.	*/

-		if (sessionBuf->delivered || sessionBuf->blockFileRef == 0)
+		/*	blockFileRef is also 0 before the file is first
+		 *	created; fileBufferPath is set only once it has.	*/
+
+		if (sessionBuf->delivered
+		|| (sessionBuf->fileBufferPath[0] != '\0'
+		&& sessionBuf->blockFileRef == 0))
 		{
 #if LTPDEBUG
 putErrmsg("Discarded late segment: block already delivered.", itoa(sessionNbr));
